# The Virtual DOM in React

The **Virtual DOM (VDOM)** is a lightweight, in-memory JavaScript representation of the actual browser DOM tree.

```javascript
// JSX:
// <div className="card"><h1>Hello</h1></div>

// Virtual DOM representation (Plain JS Object):
const vNode = {
  type: "div",
  props: {
    className: "card",
    children: [
      { type: "h1", props: { children: "Hello" } }
    ]
  }
};
```

---

## 💡 The Analogy

> Think of the Real DOM like a physical blueprint building made of bricks and mortar. Modifying the building directly every time a light switch changes is slow and expensive.
> The Virtual DOM is a 3D digital CAD model. Architects can make 1,000 simulations per second in memory, compute the exact difference, and send only the minimal brick adjustments to the construction crew.

# Component Composition in React

**Component Composition** is the practice of combining smaller, focused components together using the `children` prop or specialized slot props rather than relying on deep inheritance or bloated props.

```tsx
function Card({ header, children, footer }: {
  header?: React.ReactNode;
  children: React.ReactNode;
  footer?: React.ReactNode;
}) {
  return (
    <div className="card">
      {header && <div className="card-header">{header}</div>}
      <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
      {footer && <div className="card-footer">{footer}</div>}
    </div>
  );
}
```

# Explain useState in React

`useState` is the fundamental hook for declaring local state in functional components.

```tsx
const [count, setCount] = useState<number>(0);

// Functional updater (prevents race conditions):
setCount(prev => prev + 1);

// Lazy initialization (runs only on mount):
const [data] = useState(() => expensiveInitialComputation());
```

# Explain useEffect in React

`useEffect` lets you synchronize a component with an **external system** (such as APIs, DOM events, timers, or websockets) after rendering.

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const handler = () => console.log("Window resized");
  window.addEventListener("resize", handler);

  // Cleanup function runs before next effect execution and unmount
  return () => window.removeEventListener("resize", handler);
}, []); // Empty deps = runs once on mount
```

# Stale Closures in React

A **stale closure** occurs when an asynchronous callback or effect captures a variable from an older render cycle and continues referencing that outdated value.

```tsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      // ❌ Bug: 'count' is captured as 0 forever!
      // setCount(count + 1);

      // ✅ Fix: Use functional updater to always read fresh state
      setCount(prev => prev + 1);
    }, 1000);

    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []); // count omitted
}
```

# useMemo vs useCallback

---

## Comparison Table

| Hook | Returns | Primary Purpose |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`useMemo`** | The **cached result** of a function | Avoid expensive calculation re-runs |
| **`useCallback`** | The **cached function instance** itself | Prevent child re-renders when passing callbacks to `React.memo` |

```tsx
// useMemo caches a value:
const sortedList = useMemo(() => items.sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price), [items]);

// useCallback caches a function definition:
const handleClick = useCallback((id: string) => {
  deleteItem(id);
}, [deleteItem]);
```

# Explain useReducer in React

`useReducer` is an alternative to `useState` preferred for complex state logic involving multiple sub-values or when the next state depends on the previous state via defined actions.

```tsx
type State = { count: number };
type Action = { type: "increment" } | { type: "decrement" } | { type: "reset" };

function reducer(state: State, action: Action): State {
  switch (action.type) {
    case "increment": return { count: state.count + 1 };
    case "decrement": return { count: state.count - 1 };
    case "reset": return { count: 0 };
  }
}

const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { count: 0 });
```

# Creating Custom Hooks in React

A **custom hook** is a JavaScript function whose name starts with `use` and that can call other React hooks.

```tsx
function useDebounce<T>(value: T, delayMs: number = 300): T {
  const [debouncedValue, setDebouncedValue] = useState<T>(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setTimeout(() => setDebouncedValue(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(timer);
  }, [value, delayMs]);

  return debouncedValue;
}
```

# Lazy Loading in React

```tsx
import { lazy, Suspense } from "react";

const HeavyChart = lazy(() => import("./HeavyChart"));

function Dashboard() {
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<div>Loading Chart...</div>}>
      <HeavyChart />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

# Building Reusable Forms in React

Use modular architectures with headless libraries like **React Hook Form** + schema validation via **Zod**:

```tsx
import { useForm } from "react-hook-form";
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod";
import { z } from "zod";

const FormSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  password: z.string().min(8),
});
```

